{"t": 1786.907, "kind": "tool_call", "tool": "update_finding", "args": {"finding_id": "f_34fa4463", "description": "mIRC version installed and executed 3 times (last 2004-08-25 16:20:34). IRC log files found in Program Files/mIRC/logs/ showing the user briefly accessed multiple channels on 2004-08-20. Channel names reference elite hacking (#Elite.Hackers, #evilfork) and warez distribution (#ISO-WAREZ). However, actual IRC logs show very brief visits (most sessions under 5 minutes) with minimal participation. Available log content shows only casual chat or gibberish messages (e.g., \"glib smarm cuyk\") rather than substantive discussion of hacking techniques, exploits, or coordinated attacks. User appears to have briefly browsed these channels between 10:29 and 14:54 UTC on 2004-08-20 without engaging in detailed hacking-related conversations visible in recovered logs. While channel selection suggests interest in hacking communities, the limited actual participation and lack of discussion about specific attacks or techniques cannot be directly confirmed from available evidence.", "confidence": "inference"}, "is_error": false, "result": "[mulder audit: output blake2b:465efd09cb5e229d32d139de8c2303e96a37bb43f96b40ccf65875b732e2d8f3, 17 ms]"}
{"t": 1790.969, "kind": "tool_call", "tool": "update_finding", "args": {"finding_id": "f_92d94bd5", "description": "A packet capture file named \"interception\" (173,372 bytes) was found at Documents and Settings/Mr. Evil/interception on the disk image, indicating network traffic was captured. tcpdump analysis of the file shows 459 frames totaling 166,004 bytes captured on the Ethernet interface. Captured traffic includes 375 TCP frames, 81 UDP frames, and 3 ARP frames. HTTP protocol analysis identified 85 HTTP frames within captured traffic. IP conversation analysis shows communication between 192.168.254.2 and external IP 207.68.174.248 (262 frames, 97 KB total). Additionally, traffic to 65.54.179.195, 65.54.179.230, and 65.54.183.198 (Microsoft IP ranges). SSDP (UPnP discovery) traffic detected. The HTTP requests are to mobile.msn.com (29 requests), www.passportimages.com (5 requests), login.passport.net (5 requests), and login.passport.com (1 request) using Windows CE mobile user agent (MSIE 4.01; Windows CE; PPC; 240x320). This traffic pattern is consistent with routine Windows mobile device synchronization or browsing activity rather than targeted network interception or attack traffic capture."}, "is_error": false, "result": "[mulder audit: output blake2b:09508bed4a29e6cc7181135a086ba0939d60a53cc1d64a1f2a4eb749b633f8ec, 16 ms]"}
